. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 * 
. 
.         * This analysis tests the hypothesis that having daughters leads to m
> ore left-leaning political preferences.
. 
.         * The dependent variable is plh0004, which captures aself-placement o
> n a left–right scale). Respondents place themselves on a unidimensional left–
> right continuum. A lower score (towards “left”) indicates more left-leaning v
> iews.
. 
.         * The key independent variable is d_daughter, a dummy equal to 1 if t
> he respondent has at least one daughter, and 0 otherwise.
. 
.         * The regression is estimated using a linear OLS model, with the samp
> le restricted to the 2009, 2014, and 2019 wave, which is the only years that 
> includes both the dependent variable and the daughter indicator.
. 
.         * Control variables include respondent gender (sex), year of birth (g
> ebjahr), marital status (pgfamstd), and years of education (pgbilzeit), to ac
> count for demographic and socioeconomic differences in gender attitudes.
. 
.         * Expected finding: If having daughters increases support for more le
> ft-leaning political preferences, we expect a negative and statistically sign
> ificant coefficient on d_daughter, indicating that respondents with daughters
>  are more likely to place themselves closer to the left on the political spec
> trum.
.                 
.         *H2: 
.         reg plh0004 d_daughter sex gebjahr i.pgfamstd pgbilzeit i.syear if (p
> lh0004 != -8) & (plh0004 != -5) & (plh0004 != -1) & (pgfamstd != -3) & (pgfam
> std != -1) & (pgbilzeit != -2) & (pgbilzeit != -1), r 

